1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
It's a story of a couple

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:05,000
who wanted to have a child.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
They wanted their child to be born deaf.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,500
The reason is that both

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:16,000
of the partners were themselves deaf,

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
and they wanted a child like themselves.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
So they sought out a sperm donor.

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
They needed a sperm donor in any case,

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
because this was a lesbian couple.

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
They sought out a sperm donor who was himself deaf

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,000
and who had five generations of deafness in his family.

12
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
They conceived a child, and they succeeded.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Their child was born deaf.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
They were born deaf.

15
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
They had no other children.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
They had no other child.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
They had no other mother.

18
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
They had no other child.

19
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,500
The case of a deaf couple

20
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,000
who wanted to have a deaf child

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
raises one of the hard questions of the boundaries.

22
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
They argued that deafness,

23
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
is not a disability, but it's a distinctive identity.

24
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Now, is deafness a disability?

25
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Were they impairing the child?

26
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Or were they creating a

27
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
child with an identity like theirs?

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
That was a big debate.

29
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,000
It was a controversial case.

30
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
So we do, I think, have to

31
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
recognize that there will be borderline cases.

32
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
And we will have to

33
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
argue as a society and debate.

34
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
How to regard deafness.

35
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Or a tendency to obesity.

36
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Or baldness.

37
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Or what about teeth, straightening of teeth in orthodontia.

38
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Are these medically necessary?

39
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Or are they purely preferential?

40
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
But what this means is that

41
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
the new era of genetic technology

42
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
requires that we have a public debate

43
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
about the meaning of health,

44
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:31,000
and I would say also the limits of genetic technology.

45
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,500
I think some of the

46
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,000
most penetrating, interesting criticisms and worries

47
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
about biotechnology and genetic engineering

48
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
have come to us in popular culture,

49
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
including novels and film.

50
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Like most parents of their time,

51
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
they were determined that their next child

52
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
would come into the world in what had become

53
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
the natural way.

54
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Their extracted ovules,

55
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
the marie,

56
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
have been fertilized with Antonio's sperm.

57
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
After the exploration, we have two healthy boys

58
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
and two very healthy girls.

59
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Naturally, without any predisposition

60
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
to any hereditary disease.

61
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
All that remains is to select the most compatible candidate.

62
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
First, we can decide the sex.

63
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Have you thought about it?

64
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
You see, we would like Vincent to

65
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
have a brother to play with him.

66
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Of course. Hello, Vincent.

67
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Hello.

68
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
I think there is a tendency today

69
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:52,000
to embrace genetic technology and bioengineering,

70
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
not only for health,

71
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
where I think it's entirely appropriate,

72
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
but also to give parents more choice

73
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:06,000
about the genetic characteristics of their children.

74
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
One of the most successful sperm banks in the world

75
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
is called California Cryobank.

76
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
It's a for-profit company. They sell sperm.

77
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
And they sell sperm which is carefully calculated,

78
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,500
cataloged according to the

79
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,000
genetic characteristics of the donors.

80
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
And very few donors are accepted.

81
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
How do they choose the sperm donors?

82
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
This company.

83
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
They advertise for donors in college newspapers.

84
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
The compensation, what do you suppose the compensation is?

85
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
It's up to $900 per month.

86
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Ivy League sperm, they advertise.

87
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Not only that, they provide detailed information

88
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
about the physical characteristics of each donor,

89
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
ethnic origin, college major.

90
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Now, the company has no eugenic purpose.

91
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
They just want to make money.

92
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
It's a commercial company.

93
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
And I think that represents a danger.

94
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:23,000
And because what it does really is to turn children

95
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
and childbearing into an extension of the consumer society,

96
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
to turn children into commodities.

97
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:35,000
But in principle, what those technologies will make possible

98
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
will be an explosion of responsibility

99
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
of parents for the genetic design of their children.

100
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Yeah, now get ready for the age of designer babies.

101
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
An L.A. fertility clinic says it will soon allow parents

102
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,500
to choose traits like gender, hair

103
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:55,000
color, eye color, and skin color.

104
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Doctors there are using technology,

105
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
usually use the screen for diseases,

106
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
and there is not a single law on

107
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
the books in this country to stop them.

108
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,500
So, this is the first self-replicating

109
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:12,000
species that we've had on the planet

110
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
whose parent is a computer.

111
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
It also is the first species to have

112
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
its own website encoded in its genetic code.

113
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
So, in that case, it's possible that

114
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
children will come to hold their parents responsible

115
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,500
for their genetic characteristics, for

116
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:34,000
their height, for their physical appearance,

117
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
maybe even for their academic performance.

118
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,500
I think that would damage

119
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:42,000
the relation between parents and children,

120
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,500
and it would really be

121
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:45,000
also the commodification of the relationship

122
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
between parents and children.

123
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,000
And I think those consumer uses of genetic engineering

124
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:55,000
are morally impermissible.

125
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,500
But basically, there are two

126
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:00,000
agencies who can ultimately decide.

127
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
There is the market, which essentially

128
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
is what will make these decisions

129
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,000
if there is no public collective decision,

130
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
or there are democratic institutions.

131
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
And I hope that democratically

132
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
we will have a public debate

133
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
and a public decision taking about

134
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
the ethical limits of new biotechnology.

